# Retrieval evaluation set. One JSON object per line; lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# Copy to eval/questions.jsonl and replace the examples with your own questions.
#
#   id       unique label (optional; auto-numbered if omitted)
#   question the text you would type into the chat box
#   doc      a phrase contained in the uploaded document's filename (case-insensitive)
#   pages    page number(s) where the answer actually is  <- check these by hand in the PDF viewer
#   lang     "tr" / "en" ... (optional; produces a per-language table)
#
# Tips for a set that actually discriminates between retrieval modes (aim for 30-50 questions):
#   - Mix Turkish questions over English documents and vice versa (tests the multilingual embedding).
#   - Include exact-term questions (acronyms, template names, numbers): FTS should win these.
#   - Include paraphrased / conceptual questions that avoid the document's own wording: vectors should win these.
#   - Include a few vague questions. If every mode scores 100%, the set is too easy to prove anything.
#   - Do NOT write questions by copying a sentence from the page; that only measures string matching.
#
# The first row comes from the README's own demo (page 13 of the Software Products PDF). Verify that the
# page numbering matches `page_number` in your database before trusting the numbers.
{"id": "q01", "question": "moore vizyonu nedir", "doc": "Software Products", "pages": [13], "lang": "tr"}
